From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 14:24:14 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] show the window from linear dma-buf buffers

The receiver's window was an XRGB8888 wl_shm buffer, redrawn black
and committed on every frame, and the stream was shown by waylandsink
in a surface of its own. On the AGL cluster in a QEMU guest none of
that can go on a display plane: a shm buffer never does, and
waylandsink only has shm to give the decoder. The window sits over the
cluster, so agl-compositor composed both with the GPU on every frame.

Allocate the window's three buffers with gbm on /dev/dri/card0
(SCANOUT | LINEAR, XRGB8888) and hand them over with zwp_linux_dmabuf,
version 3 so that the LINEAR modifier is not dropped (before it the
compositor takes the buffer's modifier as implicit, and libweston
never puts such a buffer on a plane). Without a gbm device, or if the
compositor refuses the buffer, the window falls back to wl_shm.

Draw black once when the window is configured and then only when a
frame arrives: the stream ends in an appsink (BGRx, scaled to the
window's size), whose callback wakes the main loop through an
eventfd, and the main loop copies the frame, with the stride of the
buffer's own video meta, into a free buffer. A second after the last
frame the window shows black again instead of that frame; while no
buffer is free for it, it tries again a frame later. Frames of
another size are dropped, and said so once. The main loop also waits
for a full Wayland socket to drain and exits with an error when the
compositor goes away.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
